(BackgroundSalt substitutes with reduced sodium levels and increased potassium levels have been ) Tj T*
(shown to lower blood pressure, but their effects on cardiovascular and safety outcomes are ) Tj T*
(uncertain.MethodsWe conducted an open-label, cluster-randomized trial involving persons from ) Tj T*
(600 villages in rural China. The participants had a history of stroke or were 60 years of age ) Tj T*
(or older and had high blood pressure. The villages were randomly assigned in a 1:1 ratio to ) Tj T*
(the intervention group, in which the participants used a salt substitute \(75% sodium chloride ) Tj T*
(and 25% potassium chloride by mass\), or to the control group, in which the participants ) Tj T*
(continued to use regular salt \(100% sodium chloride\). The primary outcome was stroke, the ) Tj T*
(secondary outcomes were major adverse cardiovascular events and death from any cause, and the ) Tj T*
(safety outcome was clinical hyperkalemia.ResultsA total of 20,995 persons were enrolled in the ) Tj T*
(trial. The mean age of the participants was 65.4 years, and 49.5% were female, 72.6% had a ) Tj T*
(history of stroke, and 88.4% a history of hypertension. The mean duration of follow-up was ) Tj T*
(4.74 years. The rate of stroke was lower with the salt substitute than with regular salt ) Tj T*
(\(29.14 events vs. 33.65 events per 1000 person-years; rate ratio, 0.86; 95% confidence ) Tj T*
(interval [CI], 0.77 to 0.96; P = 0.006\), as were the rates of major cardiovascular events ) Tj T*
(\(49.09 events vs. 56.29 events per 1000 person-years; rate ratio, 0.87; 95% CI, 0.80 to 0.94; ) Tj T*
(PConclusionsAmong persons who had a history of stroke or were 60 years of age or older and had ) Tj T*
(high blood pressure, the rates of stroke, major cardiovascular events, and death from any ) Tj T*
(cause were lower with the salt substitute than with regular salt. \(Funded by the National ) Tj T*
(Health and Medical Research Council of Australia; SSaSS ClinicalTrials.gov number, ) Tj T*
(NCT02092090.\).) Tj T*
